## Privacy — learning capture

{% if capture_mode != "off" %}Learning capture is **ON for this install** (`{{capture_mode}}`, chosen at init): a background {{ provider.name.agent }}
job reads your session transcript and changed files to record durable learnings under
`state://agent-memory/` (a committed store). It skips secret-bearing files (`.env`, `*.pem`/`*.key`,
`credentials.*`) and redacts secret-shaped values, but transcripts can still hold sensitive context —
**review new `agent-memory/` entries before committing them**. Controls:

- `{{ provider.environment.kit_prefix }}_NO_AUTOCAPTURE=1` — disable capture entirely.
- `{{ provider.environment.kit_prefix }}_CAPTURE_MAX_LINES` / `{{ provider.environment.kit_prefix }}_CAPTURE_MAX_BYTES` — bound what each run feeds the job.
- `{{ provider.executable.cli }} privacy-report` — audit every installed hook: what it reads/writes, whether a
  background job spawns, and how to disable it.
{% else %}Learning capture is **OFF for this install** (capture is opt-in — no background job reads your
session transcripts). Recall still works: learnings already in `state://agent-memory/` load at
session start. To enable capture, re-run `{{ provider.executable.cli }} init` interactively (or set `capture_mode:` in
a `--config` file), then audit the result with `{{ provider.executable.cli }} privacy-report`.
{% endif %}

## Start the workflow

Open the project in {{ provider.name.host }} and run the pipeline entry point:

```
command://sdlc Build JWT authentication for the backend
```

The orchestrator asks a few ordered questions, classifies the work, then delegates to specialist
agents through the pipeline phases — enforcing a quality gate between each. With this profile the
active gates are: **{{ profile_label }}**.

You can also invoke any single skill directly (e.g. `command://spec-driven-development`, `command://code-review-and-quality`)
or ask {{ provider.name.agent }} to use a specific agent.

The versioned headless-loop interface ships at `{{ provider.path.loop_script }}`, but new unattended
iterations currently fail closed before a host process starts. Portable process groups cannot prove
containment of deliberately detached descendants, so the kit does not mint an automated gate-
transition token. Use the interactive lifecycle; the script can still strictly validate an already-
completed run. See the autonomous-operation guide before relying on a future promotion.

## Core rules (non-negotiable)

- Never read or print secrets. Never run destructive commands without confirmation.
- Plan before large edits; write a spec before implementing a feature.
- Run the project's validation (lint, type-check, tests) before declaring work complete.

See `{{ provider.path.project_instructions }}` and `{{ provider.path.rules }}` for the full set.

## Extending the config

- **Add a stack** (frontend framework, backend language/framework, or database): add an entry to the
  {{ provider.executable.cli }} catalog and a `templates/stacks/<stack_dir>/` folder with overlay rules — then re-run
  `{{ provider.executable.cli }} init`. It's a data change, not code.
- **Add a skill / agent:** drop a `{{ provider.path.skills }}<name>/SKILL.md` or `{{ provider.path.agents }}<name>.md`.
- **Enable MCP later:** add servers to `{{ provider.path.mcp_config }}` (see `{{ provider.executable.cli }} list-options` for the catalog).
- **Upgrade safely:** `{{ provider.executable.cli }} diff` to preview, then `{{ provider.executable.cli }} upgrade` (your edits are backed
  up, never silently overwritten). `{{ provider.executable.cli }} doctor` checks config health.

